<tr><td>java Test A B C</td><td>A / B / C</td></tr>
<tr><td>java Test A B</td><td>A / B</td></tr>
<tr><td>java Test</td><td>no output (loop body never runs)</td></tr>
</table>
<table>
<tr><th>Command</th><th>Output</th></tr>
<tr><td>java Test A B C</td><td>A / B / C</td></tr>
<tr><td>java Test A B</td><td>A / B</td></tr>
<tr><td>java Test</td><td>no output</td></tr>
</table>
<table>
<tr><th>Piece</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td>String[]</td><td>the type of main’s parameter — always an array of Strings</td></tr>
<tr><td>Parameter name (args, ax, …)</td><td>only a local name; JVM does not care</td></tr>
<tr><td>args.length</td><td>how many tokens were passed (0 if you typed only java Test)</td></tr>
<tr><td>args[i]</td><td>the i-th token, as a String (even if it looks like a number)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Empty invocation</td><td>args is a length-0 array, not null</td></tr>
<tr><td>+ on two slots</td><td>concatenation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Need a number</td><td>Integer.parseInt(args[i]) (or similar)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Need a space inside one token</td><td>&quot;double quotes&quot; on the command line</td></tr>
</table>
</body></html>
